  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWarm and dry from this weekend  through Tuesday of next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nNorthern stream shortwave ridging crosses the area Saturday,\nfollowed by west southwest flow aloft through Saturday evening.\nThe result should be dry conditions across the region, with\nhighs on Saturday around 10 degrees above normal.  A northern \nstream shortwave crosses the area late Saturday night and Sunday\nmorning, bringing a slight chance of showers across northern \nportions of Aroostook, Somerset, and Piscatiquis counties. Any \nrainfall should be light, with amounts less than a tenth of an \ninch expected. Sunday will not quite be as warm as Saturday with\na passage of a surface cold front ahead of the northern stream \nshortwave. Highs on Sunday should be around 5-10 degrees above \nnormal. Highs on Saturday and Sunday were calculated using a \nblend of ConsMOS, ConsRaw and NBM50. With dry air in the lower\nlevels and mixing sufficient to tap that drier air anticipated,\nlowered the dewpoints Saturday and Sunday as well with a blend\nof mixing down the dewpoints from aloft and NBM10. Wind gusts on\nSunday behind the cold front could get up to 25-30mph, with\nminimum relative humidities generally in the 30%s Sunday\nafternoon. Depending on fuel moistures there could be some fire\nweather concerns as a result. \n\nDeep layered ridging builds in Sunday night through Tuesday. It\nshould be dry at least through Monday evening. For late Monday\nnight, where a surface to 850 mb warm front lifting through the\nregion stalls out by Tuesday morning will determine if the \nCrown of Maine sees any showers late Monday night. Depending on\nhow fast this front then turns around and pushes south as a cold\nfront, along with how successful any shortwaves are in making \nit over the top of the ridge will determine if Tuesday stays dry\nor if there are rounds of showers across the region. Highs\nMonday and Tuesday were derived by lowering NBM temperatures a \ncouple of degrees. Dewpoints were lowered on Monday as well. \nEven with afternoon relative humidities progged into the 20s on \nMonday, wind gusts should peak at 10-15 mph - with some areas \nhaving gusts stay under 10 mph, so any fire weather concerns \nwould be less than on Sunday.",
